
{"id":"5601b5cb-f352-4830-a149-f5053867e6d2","object":"chat.completion","created":1785025843,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing required fields')\\n\\n    nodes_raw = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n\\n    # Copy nodes and build mappings\\n    nodes = {node['id']: dict(node) for node in nodes_raw}\\n    parent = {nid: nodes[nid]['parent'] for nid in nodes}\\n    children = {nid: set() for nid in nodes}\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for nid, node in nodes.items():\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        p = node['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            if p not in children:\\n                children[p] = set()\\n            children[p].add(nid)\\n\\n    # Validate required symbols exist\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in symbol_counts or symbol_counts[sym] == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\\\")\\n\\n    removed = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    # Precompute sorted list of node ids by depth desc, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-nodes[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n\\n    changed = True\\n    while changed:\\n        changed = False\\n        for nid in sorted_nodes:\\n            if nid in removed:\\n                continue\\n            node = nodes[nid]\\n            if not node['removable']:\\n                continue\\n            if node['parent'] is None:  # root\\n                continue\\n            # Check if node has any children (among non-removed nodes)\\n            if children[nid]:\\n                continue\\n            # Check required symbol\\n            sym = node['symbol']\\n            if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts[sym] <= 1:\\n                continue\\n            # Remove this node\\n            removed.add(nid)\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            # Decrement symbol count\\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n            # Remove node from its parent's children\\n            p = node['parent']\\n            if p in children:\\n                children[p].discard(nid)\\n            changed = True\\n\\n    # Build retained set\\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed])\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements a \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" algorithm based on the description. The input case has \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list. Each node has id, parent (null for root), symbol, depth, removable (bool), utility (float). The objective: reduce the tree by removing some removable nodes, but retain required symbols, and ensure grammar validity (likely meaning that after removal, the tree structure is still valid? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But given limited info, we need to infer: we have a tree (parent pointers). Each node has a symbol. \"required_symbols\" is a list of symbols that must remain present (i.e., at least one node with that symbol must remain). \"removable\" indicates if the node can be removed. The root is not removable. We need to decide which removable nodes to remove to reduce the tree while preserving required symbols. Also \"grammar_valid\" might mean that after removal, the tree must still be a valid parse tree according to some grammar, but we don't have grammar rules. Possibly \"grammar_valid\" means that the child-parent relationships are maintained? Actually, if we remove a node, its children might need to be reattached to its parent? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". In Perses, reduction often involves deleting nodes and reconnecting children. But here we only have \"removable\" flag. We need to understand what \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" implies. Possibly we are to consider removing removable nodes one by one, in order of depth deeper first, and then by some tie-breaker (like utility? but they said \"use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\"). And we must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present (i.e., there exists at least one node with each required symbol). Also \"grammar_valid\" might mean that the tree structure is still a valid parse tree (i.e., each node's children are still valid according to grammar). Without grammar rules, maybe we assume any tree is valid as long as parent relationships are consistent? Or perhaps we need to ensure that after removal, the parent-child relationships are still valid given the symbols. But we don't have production rules. Alternatively, maybe \"grammar_valid\" is just a check that the tree is a tree (no cycles, each node has a parent except root) which is already satisfied. Or maybe \"grammar_valid\" refers to the fact that each node's symbol is consistent with its parent? But no grammar given.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I need to read the instructions carefully: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\", \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we are simulating Perses reduction. In Perses, you have a grammar and a parse tree; you try to delete nodes or replace subtrees with smaller ones while preserving interestingness (i.e., the reduced program still triggers the same behavior). Here interestingness is represented by \"required_symbols\"? They mention \"interestingness oracle\". The required_symbols might be the set of symbols that must be present for the program to be interesting. So we need to preserve that at least one node of each required symbol exists.\n\nAlso \"grammar_valid\" means the resulting tree must be a valid parse tree according to the grammar. Since we don't have grammar, perhaps the input is already a valid parse tree, and we only remove nodes that are \"removable\" if the resulting tree is still a valid parse tree under some implicit grammar constraints. What constraints? Possibly: a node can only be removed if its removal does not create an invalid parent-child combination (like a child having a symbol that is not allowed under the parent). Without production rules, we can't check. But maybe the problem is designed such that the only constraint is that the parent of the removed node's children becomes the removed node's parent, and that this is always allowed (since we don't know grammar). Or maybe removal is only allowed if the removed node's children are all leaves? Not sure.\n\nLet's look at the fixture payloads. They have nodes with parents forming a tree. In all cases, the root has symbol \"root\". Required symbols are [\"root\", \"s1\"]. So we need to keep at least one node with symbol \"s1\". Look at the nodes: n1 has symbol s1, depth 1, not removable. n4 has symbol s1, depth 2, removable. So if we remove n4, symbol s1 still exists (n1). n6 has symbol s0, removable. n2 has symbol s2, removable. n3 has symbol s0, not removable. n5 has symbol s2, not removable. So likely we can remove n4 and n6 and n2? But removing n2 would remove its child n4 (if we remove n2, its child n4 might also be removed? Unless children are reattached? The description says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". Typically, removing a node means deleting that node and reconnecting its children to its parent. So if we remove n2, its child n4 would become child of root. That might be allowed. But then we need to check grammar validity: is \"s1\" allowed under \"root\"? In the original tree, root has children n1 (s1), n2 (s2), n3 (s0). So s1 is allowed under root because n1 is child. So after reattaching n4 (s1) to root, it would be fine. Similarly, removing n6 (s0 under n1) would move n6's children? n6 has no children (none listed with parent n6). So just remove it. Removing n2 would move n4 to root. That might be allowed.\n\nBut the problem asks to implement \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" and \"consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\". Also \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Wait: \"never remove ... a node with retained children\". That means if a node has children that we are not removing, we cannot remove that node (because children would be orphaned? Actually if we remove node, children are reconnected, so they are retained but children are reattached? The phrase \"retained children\" might mean children that are kept (not removed). In Perses, you can remove a node and its children become children of its parent, so the children are retained. So that condition might mean you cannot remove a node if it would cause its children to be removed (if children are not retained)? But typically you remove a node and its children are reattached, so they are retained. The phrase \"a node with retained children\" might mean that if the node has children that are not being removed, then you cannot remove the node because that would delete the children? Actually, if the node is removed, its children become children of the parent, so they survive. So why would \"retained children\" prevent removal? Maybe it means that if the node has children that are themselves retained (i.e., we decide to keep them), then we cannot remove the node because the children would need to be reattached and might violate grammar? Or perhaps the interpretation is that we should not remove a node that has any children that are themselves removable and we might remove later? The phrasing is ambiguous.\n\nGiven the complexity and time, I need to produce a plausible implementation that follows the specified rules: use depth (deeper first), tie-break by node ID. Check required symbols. Possibly also check \"grammar_valid\" using some simple rule like: after removal, the parent-child symbol pairs must match some allowed combinations from the original tree? But we only have one case.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the solution is just: for each removable node in order of descending depth, then by node ID (ascending?), try removing it temporarily; if after removal all required symbols are still present, then permanently remove it. Otherwise, keep it. And \"grammar_valid\" is automatically true because we only delete nodes (not subtrees) and reattach children? But we don't do reattachment in this simple algorithm; we just remove the node entirely, which in a tree reduction often means deleting the node and its children? In Perses, there are different strategies: deletion of a node and its subtree, or replacement with a smaller subtree. The instructions mention \"deletion and replacement candidates\". But the problem says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So removal is a candidate; we remove the node (and presumably its children? Or just the node and children are reattached?). The output expects \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". So we need to decide which nodes to remove.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is: we process nodes in order of decreasing depth (deepest first), then by node ID as tie-breaker (maybe increasing). For each node that is removable, we check if removing it (i.e., deleting it from the tree, and reconnecting its children to its parent) would still keep all required symbols present. If yes, we mark it for removal. Then we proceed. At the end, we output the IDs of removed nodes (in the order they were removed? The output schema just says arrays, not ordered, but the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to return removed_node_ids in the order