t is removable (and not root), tentatively remove it (i.e., consider the tree with that node removed and its children reattached to its parent). Check: 1) After removal, the tree is still connected? Yes, because children get new parent. 2) Does there exist at least one node for each required symbol? Compute set of symbols of nodes still present (including the reattached children). 3) Is the root still present? Root is never removed because it's not removable. If conditions satisfied, mark node as removed and update the tree data structure to reflect removal. Otherwise, keep node. Continue.\n\nWe need to maintain a mutable representation of the tree. Since we cannot mutate input, we make a copy. We need to track parent relationships. Use a dict node_id -> node dict, and also maybe children list for quick reattachment. Simpler: keep list of nodes and each node has parent id. When we remove a node, for each child of that node (i.e., nodes with parent equal to this node's id), we set their parent to this node's parent. Then we remove this node from the list (or mark as removed). Then later nodes may refer to removed parent. We need to ensure we don't process removed nodes.\n\nAlso we need to compute symbol representation: after each removal, we can recalculate symbols present among nodes that are not removed.\n\nWe must use only standard library.\n\nEdge cases: node with no children removal is straightforward.\n\nThe result: removed_node_ids list in order of removal; retained_node_ids sorted (maybe alphabetically). For retained_node_ids, we include all nodes not removed, including root and all kept nodes.\n\nNow, the output must be JSON with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". So implement solve(case) that returns that dict.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The \"required_symbols\" list may include symbols that are not present? But input is presumably valid. The root symbol is always \"root\". We should handle cases where no valid reduction possible? But likely always