t we should not remove a node if any of its children are not being removed (i.e., we intend to keep them). Because that would change the tree structure and potentially break grammar. In Perses, you often delete nodes that have no children (terminals) or after removing children. So the algorithm might be: process nodes from leaves upward. When we remove a leaf, its parent may later be considered if all its children are removed. That would be consistent with \"consider deeper nodes first\". So we should only consider a node for removal if it has no children that are still present (i.e., all its children have been removed already). That would satisfy \"never remove a node with retained children\". That is a plausible interpretation. Let's check the fixture: n6 is a leaf (no children), removable. n4 is leaf (no children), removable. n2 has child n4, removable. If we remove n4 first (depth 2), then n2 becomes leaf (no remaining children) and can be removed later. That matches the algorithm. n3 has child n5 (not removable), so n3 cannot be removed because it has a non-removable child (retained). That is correct. So the rule: we can only remove a node if all of its children have been removed (or are also being removed in the same step? But we process one at a time, so we need to remove children first). So we should only consider a node for removal if it currently has no children that are not removed (i.e., all its children are either already removed or have no children? Actually, we need to check if it has any children in the current tree (after previous removals). If it has any children, we cannot remove it (because those children would become orphans? No, they would be reattached. But maybe grammar forbids that). The condition \"a node with retained children\" explicitly says we never remove such a node. So we will enforce: a node can be removed only if it has no children (i.e., it's a leaf) after considering previous removals. That makes sense.\n\nThus, our algorithm: process nodes in descending depth, but only consider nodes that are leaves (no children in the current tree). When we remove a node, its children (if any) would have already been removed; but if it has no children, it's a leaf. So we need to maintain current children relationships. After removing a node, its parent's children set changes (the node is removed). So we need to update.\n\nLet's implement:\n- Maintain a dict parent: node_id -> parent_id (or None for root).\n- Maintain a dict children: node_id -> set of child node ids.\n- Initially populate from list of nodes.\n- For processing order: we need to sort nodes by depth descending, but only consider those that are currently leaves. However, after removals, some nodes may become leaves. To efficiently decide, we can iterate over the sorted list and check if the node currently has any children (in the children dict) that have not been removed. If it does, skip (cannot remove). Otherwise, attempt removal.\n\nBut the order of iteration should be fixed to ensure determinism. We'll iterate over the sorted list of all nodes (by depth desc, id). For each node, if it's already removed, skip. If not removable, skip. If it has any children in the current children dict (excluding removed nodes? we keep children dict updated), skip. Then tentatively remove: check required symbols. If passes, update data: remove this node from its parent's children set; for each child of this node (but it has no children because we skip if children exist, so no children to reattach). Actually, if we only remove leaves, there are no children to reattach. So the tree structure simply deletes that leaf. That is simpler. So we don't need to reattach children; we are just deleting leaf nodes. That matches \"never remove a node with retained children\". So we are only removing leaf nodes that are removable. That is a simple reduction strategy: remove removable leaves one by one, preserving required symbols. This is likely the intended behavior.\n\nThus, implement as:\n- Compute leaf condition: node has no children (i.e., no other node has parent = this node's id) among nodes that are not removed.\n- If leaf and removable and symbol check passes, remove it.\n\nAfter removal, we update children sets: remove this node from its parent's children set.\n\nContinue iterating; after some removals, other nodes may become leaves (if their children were all removed). Since we process in descending depth, deeper nodes (leaves) get removed first, then shallower nodes that become leaves later.\n\nThis should produce a deterministic result.\n\nNow, we must check required symbols: after each removal, we need to ensure that for each symbol in required_symbols, there exists at least one node (not removed) with that symbol. We can maintain a count of symbols per symbol. Initially count symbols. When we remove a node, decrement its symbol count. If after decrement, any required symbol count becomes 0, then we cannot remove that node. So we need to restore. Efficient: before removal, check if the node's symbol count is >1? Not exactly, because multiple nodes may have same symbol. We need to check if after removal, all required symbols still have count >=1. So we can precompute counts, and for candidate node, if its symbol is required and the count of that symbol is exactly 1, then removal would eliminate it, so reject. Also for other required symbols unaffected, they are fine. So condition: either node's symbol not in required_symbols, or count of that symbol > 1.\n\nThat check is sufficient because we only remove one node at a time.\n\nNow, implement.\n\nWe'll maintain:\n- nodes_by_id: dict id -> node dict (original copy, but we will not modify them except we keep a set of removed ids).\n- parent dict: node_id -> parent_id\n- children dict: node_id -> set of child ids\n- symbol_counts: dict symbol -> int\n\nInitialize. Then iterate over sorted list of node ids (by depth desc, id). For each:\n   - if id in removed: continue\n   - if not node['removable']: continue\n   - if node['parent'] is None: continue (root)\n   - if len(children[id]) > 0: continue (has children)\n   - symbol = node['symbol']\n   - if symbol in required_symbols and symbol_counts[symbol] == 1: continue (removing would eliminate required symbol)\n   - else: remove it:\n        - add id to removed set\n        - decrement symbol_counts[symbol]\n        - remove id from children[parent] set\n        - optionally, we don't need to update children of this node because it had no children.\n   - record removal order.\n\nAfter loop, all removable nodes that can be removed are processed. However, there could be nodes that become leaves later but were skipped earlier because they had children initially. Since we iterate over the sorted list once, we might miss them. The algorithm should re-process or iterate multiple times. Typically, in such reduction, you repeatedly scan until no more removals. So we should use a loop that repeats until no changes. We can use a while True: for each node in sorted order, try remove; if any removal, continue; else break. But we must be careful to keep deterministic order: each pass uses the same sorted order iteration. That should work.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a priority queue of nodes ordered by depth desc, but queue would require reinsertion. Simpler: use while loop with pass.\n\nWe'll implement: while True: removed_any = False; for each node in sorted_nodes: ... if removed, set removed_any=True. After loop, if not removed_any: break.\n\nNow, after finishing, we have removed_node_ids (list in order they were removed). retained_node_ids = all node ids not in removed set, sorted (e.g., sorted alphabetically).\n\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nTest with given fixtures mentally.\n\nCase 0:\nnodes: root (depth0, not removable), n1 (depth1, s1, not removable), n2 (depth1, s2, removable), n3 (depth1, s0, not removable), n4 (depth2, s1, removable, parent n2), n5 (depth2, s2, not removable, parent n3), n6 (depth2, s0, removable, parent n1).\nrequired: root, s1.\nInitial children: root: n1,n2,n3; n1: n6; n2: n4; n3: n5; n4: none; n5: none; n6: none.\nsymbol counts: root:1, s1:2 (n1,n4), s2:2 (n2,n5), s0:2 (n3,n6). Required symbols: root(1)>0, s1(2)>0.\nSorted by depth desc then id: depth2 nodes: n4 (id n4), n5 (n5), n6 (n6). depth1: n1,n2,n3. depth0: root.\nIterate pass1:\n- n4: removable? yes, parent n2, children none. Symbol s1. s1 count=2 (>1) so can remove. Remove n4. symbol_count[s1]=1. children[n2] removes n4. removed = {n4}, removal_order=[n4].\n- n5: removable? false (not removable), skip.\n- n6: removable? yes, children none. Symbol s0, not required, so can remove. Remove n6. symbol_count[s0]=1. children[n1] removes n6. removed={n4,n6}, removal_order=[n4,n6].\n- n1: not removable, skip.\n- n2: removable? yes, children now none (after removing n4). leaf. Symbol s2, not required. Can remove. Remove n2. symbol_count[s2]=1. children[root] removes n2. removed={n4,n6,n2}, order=[n4,n6,n2].\n- n3: not removable, skip.\n- root: skip.\nNo more changes in pass1. Now pass2: iterate again:\n- n4 removed, skip.\n- n5 not removable, skip.\n- n6 removed, skip.\n- n1 not removable, skip.\n- n2 removed, skip.\n- n3 not removable.\n- root skip.\nNo changes, break.\nRemoved: n4,n6,n2. Retained: root,n1,n3,n5. Sorted retained ids: n1,n3,n5,root? root is string \"root\", n1,n3,n5 < root? \"n1\" < \"root\"? lexicographically 'n' (110) vs 'r' (114), so \"n1\" < \"root\". So sorted: n1, n3, n5, root. That seems plausible.\n\nNow check fixture output? Not given, but we can compare with case expectation. The problem is to implement the function, not to match exact expected outputs. The fixture shows inputs only.\n\nThus, code should work.\n\nNow we must ensure we handle edge cases: if after all removals, some required symbol becomes missing? Our algorithm checks before removal, so it won't happen. If it's impossible to preserve required symbols (e.g., only one node for a required symbol and it's removable), then we would never remove it, and that node remains. That's fine.\n\nNow, we need to implement solve(case) that raises ValueError for infeasible cases. What constitutes infeasible? Possibly if required_symbols contains a symbol not present at all in initial nodes? We'll check and raise.\n\nAlso, if after processing, there are still removable nodes that could not be removed due to required symbol constraint, that's okay.\n\nNow, ensure we don't mutate case. We'll copy node dicts as needed.\n\nWrite Python code.\n\nWe'll define:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required fields\")\n\n    nodes_raw = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n\n    # Copy nodes and build mappings\n    nodes = {node['id']: dict(node) for node in nodes_raw}  # use copy\n    parent = {nid: nodes[nid]['parent'] for nid in nodes}\n    children = {nid: set() for nid in nodes}\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for nid, node in nodes.items():\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        p = node['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            if p not in children:\n                children[p] = set()\n            children[p].add(nid)\n\n    # Validate required symbols exist\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in symbol_counts or symbol_counts[sym] == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' not present\")\n\n    removed = set()\n    removed_order = []\n\n    # Precompute sorted list of node ids by depth desc, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-nodes[nid]['depth'], nid))\n\n    changed = True\n    while changed:\n        changed = False\n        for nid in sorted_nodes:\n            if nid in removed:\n                continue\n            node = nodes[nid]\n            if not node['removable']:\n                continue\n            if node['parent'] is None:  # root\n                continue\n            # Check if node has any children (among non-removed nodes)\n            # Children set includes ids that may be removed; we need to check only non-removed children.\n            # Since we update children when removing, children set should only contain non-removed children if we remove from set.\n            if children[nid]:  # if any child still present\n                continue\n            # Check required symbol\n            sym = node['symbol']\n            if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts[sym] <= 1:\n                # Cannot remove the last instance of a required symbol\n                continue\n            # Remove this node\n            removed.add(nid)\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n            # Decrement symbol count\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n            # Remove node from its parent's children\n            p = node['parent']\n            if p in children:\n                children[p].discard(nid)\n            # No need to update children of this node because it had no children\n            changed = True\n            # Note: we do not remove from sorted_nodes list; we skip removed ids.\n\n    # Build retained set\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed])\n\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids\n    }\n\nTest with given case. It should produce removed_order: n4, n6, n2. retained: n1, n3, n5, root. But note: root is \"root\". In sorted list, \"root\" will come after all \"n*\" because 'r' > 'n'. So sorted: n1, n3, n5, root. That matches.\n\nNow, we must output the code as a JSON string in the \"implementation\" key. The response must be exactly one JSON object with \"implementation\" key containing the escaped Python source. Ensure no extra whitespace. Use json.dumps to escape prope